>  Parallelization Workflows

● Parallelization Workflows

  Parallelization runs multiple LLM calls or agents simultaneously, then combines results. Useful when tasks are
  independent and speed matters.

  ---
  Two Main Variants

  1. Sectioning

  Split a task into independent subtasks, run them in parallel, merge results.

                      ┌─── Agent A: analyze sentiment ───┐
  Input (long doc) ───┤─── Agent B: extract entities  ───┼──► Merge ──► Final Output
                      └─── Agent C: summarize         ───┘

  2. Voting (Ensemble)

  Run the same task multiple times, aggregate results for higher confidence.

                      ┌─── LLM call 1 ──► answer A ───┐
  Same Input ─────────┤─── LLM call 2 ──► answer B ───┼──► Vote/Pick best
                      └─── LLM call 3 ──► answer C ───┘

  ---
  Code Example (Python — Sectioning)

  import asyncio
  import anthropic

  client = anthropic.Anthropic()

  async def analyze(task: str, content: str) -> str:
      # Run in thread pool since SDK is sync
      loop = asyncio.get_event_loop()
      response = await loop.run_in_executor(
          None,
          lambda: client.messages.create(
              model="claude-sonnet-4-6",
              max_tokens=500,
              messages=[{"role": "user", "content": f"{task}\n\n{content}"}]
          )
      )
      return response.content[0].text

  async def parallel_analyze(document: str):
      tasks = [
          analyze("Summarize this document in 3 bullet points:", document),
          analyze("Extract all named entities (people, places, orgs):", document),
          analyze("What is the overall sentiment? Rate 1-10:", document),
      ]

      summary, entities, sentiment = await asyncio.gather(*tasks)

      return {
          "summary": summary,
          "entities": entities,
          "sentiment": sentiment
      }

  # Run
  result = asyncio.run(parallel_analyze("Your long document here..."))

  ---
  Code Example (Voting)

  async def vote(question: str, num_votes: int = 3) -> str:
      tasks = [
          analyze("Answer this question:", question)
          for _ in range(num_votes)
      ]
      answers = await asyncio.gather(*tasks)

      # Ask a final LLM to pick the best/most consistent answer
      combined = "\n".join(f"Answer {i+1}: {a}" for i, a in enumerate(answers))
      best = await analyze(
          "Pick the most accurate and consistent answer from these options. Return only the best answer:",
          combined
      )
      return best

  ---
  When to Use

  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────┐
  │                  Scenario                  │  Pattern   │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ Long document with multiple analysis needs │ Sectioning │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ High-stakes decisions needing accuracy     │ Voting     │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ Independent subtasks with no dependencies  │ Sectioning │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ Reducing hallucination risk                │ Voting     │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────┤
  │ Speed optimization on large inputs         │ Sectioning │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────┘

  ---
  Key Tradeoffs

  ┌─────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │             Pro             │                   Con                   │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Much faster than sequential │ Higher token cost (runs multiple calls) │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Higher accuracy (voting)    │ Merge/aggregation logic adds complexity │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Easy to scale               │ Harder to debug when one branch fails   │
  └─────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Real-World Example Flow

  User uploads 50-page report
          │
          ▼
  Split into 10 chunks
          │
     ┌────┴────┐
     │Parallel │  ← 10 agents summarize chunks simultaneously
     └────┬────┘
          │
     Merge summaries
          │
     Final synthesis LLM call
          │
          ▼
     Single coherent summary (10x faster than sequential)

  ---
  The key insight: parallelization trades cost for speed and/or accuracy. Use it when subtasks are truly independent —
  introducing dependencies breaks the parallel benefit.

