> explain routing workflows

● Routing Workflows

  ---
  What Is It?

  Routing is a workflow pattern where an input is classified first, then directed to the most appropriate handler based
  on that classification.

                      ┌──► Handler A
  Input ──► Router ───┤
                      ├──► Handler B
                      │
                      └──► Handler C

  The router decides which path to take. Each handler is specialized for its specific type of input.

  ---
  Why Routing?

  Different inputs need different handling. A single generic prompt handles everything poorly. Specialized handlers
  handle their specific case well.

  Without Routing (Bad)

  "Handle this customer message — it could be a complaint,
  a billing question, a technical issue, or a compliment.
  Do the right thing."
  - One prompt tries to cover everything
  - Generic, mediocre responses
  - Hard to improve one case without affecting others

  With Routing (Good)

  Classify → Complaint    → Complaint handler (empathetic, escalation logic)
          → Billing       → Billing handler  (account lookup, refund logic)
          → Technical     → Tech handler     (troubleshooting steps)
          → Compliment    → Compliment handler (warm, brief acknowledgment)
  - Each handler is expert in its domain
  - Easy to improve one route independently
  - Clear separation of concerns

  ---
  Anatomy of a Router

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                  ROUTER                  │
  │                                          │
  │  1. Receive input                        │
  │  2. Classify it into a category          │
  │  3. Return the category label            │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────┘
            │           │           │
            ▼           ▼           ▼
        Handler A   Handler B   Handler C
       (specialist) (specialist) (specialist)

  The router itself is simple and focused — it only classifies, it does not handle.

  ---
  Real World Example — Customer Support

  User message: "I was charged twice for my subscription"

          ▼

  Router classifies: BILLING

          ▼

  Billing Handler:
    - Acknowledges the double charge
    - Asks for account details
    - Explains refund process
    - Offers escalation if needed

  If the same message went to the Technical handler, it would respond with troubleshooting steps — completely wrong.

  ---
  Types of Routing

  1. LLM-Based Routing

  The classifier is an LLM call. Best for nuanced, ambiguous inputs.

  Input → [LLM: what category is this?] → category → handler

  Flexible but slower and costs tokens.

  2. Rule-Based Routing

  Simple keyword or logic rules decide the route. Best for clear, predictable inputs.

  Input → if "refund" in text → billing handler
        → if "error" in text  → tech handler
        → else                → general handler

  Fast, cheap, but brittle for complex inputs.

  3. Embedding-Based Routing

  Input is compared to example embeddings. Best for semantic similarity matching.

  Input → vectorize → find closest category → handler

  Good balance of speed and flexibility.

  ---
  Multi-Level Routing

  Routes can have sub-routes — a tree of specialization.

                           ┌──► Refund request
  Input ──► Billing ───────┤
            handler        ├──► Incorrect charge
                           └──► Upgrade question

                           ┌──► Login issues
  Input ──► Technical ─────┤
            handler        ├──► App crashes
                           └──► Performance issues

  Each level narrows the specialization further.

  ---
  Routing with Fallback

  Always handle the case where the router is uncertain.

  Input ──► Router
                │
      ┌─────────┼─────────┐
      ▼         ▼         ▼
    Billing   Technical  [Unknown]
                             │
                             ▼
                       Fallback handler
                     (general, asks user
                      to clarify intent)

  Never let an unmatched input fall into silence or a wrong handler.

  ---
  Routing + Chaining Together

  Routing decides the path. Chaining handles the steps within that path.

  Input
    │
    ▼
  Router
    │
    ├──► Route A ──► [Step 1] → [Step 2] → [Step 3] → Output A
    │
    ├──► Route B ──► [Step 1] → [Step 2] → Output B
    │
    └──► Route C ──► [Step 1] → Output C

  This is how most real-world pipelines are built — routing at the top, chaining within each route.

  ---
  Where Routing Breaks Down

  ┌────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │            Problem             │              What Happens               │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Misclassification              │ Input goes to wrong handler, bad output │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Overlapping categories         │ Router is uncertain, picks wrong route  │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Too many routes                │ Router becomes complex and error-prone  │
  ├────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Input fits multiple categories │ Router must pick one, may lose nuance   │
  └────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Solutions:
  - Keep categories mutually exclusive and exhaustive
  - Add a confidence check — low confidence → fallback
  - Log all classifications to monitor misroutes
  - Allow multi-label routing when input spans categories

  ---
  Multi-Label Routing

  When an input genuinely belongs to multiple categories, handle all of them.

  Message: "I was charged twice and now my app won't open"

  Router classifies: BILLING + TECHNICAL

            ▼                    ▼
     Billing handler      Technical handler
            │                    │
            └──────┬─────────────┘
                   ▼
            Combined response

  Parallel execution, merged result.

  ---
  Key Design Principles

  1. Router only classifies — never handles, never does extra work
  2. Categories must be clear — ambiguous categories cause misroutes
  3. Always have a fallback — unknown inputs must go somewhere sensible
  4. Log everything — misclassifications are silent failures without logging
  5. Test edge cases — inputs that sit on the boundary between categories

  ---
  Routing vs Chaining vs Parallelization

  ┌─────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │     Pattern     │          Structure           │                 Best For                 │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Chaining        │ A → B → C                    │ Sequential steps, one path               │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Routing         │ A → one of B, C, D           │ Different inputs need different handling │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Parallelization │ A → B + C + D simultaneously │ Same input, multiple analyses            │
  └─────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Summary

  Routing classifies input first, then sends it to the best-suited handler. It replaces one generic prompt that does
  everything poorly with multiple specialized prompts that each do one thing well. The router is the traffic cop — it
  doesn't do the work, it directs who should.

