  ---
  Scenario 4: Medical Records Processing

  As a Workflow

  Processing lab results for a hospital.

  Lab result arrives
          │
          ▼
  Step 1: Extract patient ID and test type
          │
          ▼
  Step 2: Pull patient history from records
          │
          ▼
  Step 3: Compare result against normal ranges
          │
        ┌─┴──┐
     Normal  Abnormal
        │       │
        ▼       ▼
    Archive   Flag for
    result    doctor review
          │
          ▼
  Step 4: Generate summary report
          │
          ▼
  Step 5: Notify relevant physician

  Why workflow here?
  - Life-critical — must be predictable
  - Same structure for every result
  - Fully auditable for compliance
  - No room for improvisation

  As an Agent

  A clinical research assistant analyzing a complex patient case.

  Agent receives: "Patient has 3 years of conflicting
  diagnoses, 12 specialists, and worsening symptoms.
  Review full history and suggest next steps."

  Agent decides:
    → Read all clinical notes (tool call)
    → Identifies 4 conflicting diagnoses
    → Searches medical literature for overlap (tool call)
    → Finds rare autoimmune condition matching symptoms
    → Cross-checks all medications for interactions (tool call)
    → Finds one combination contraindicated for this condition
    → Reviews similar cases in research database (tool call)
    → Synthesizes findings into clinical summary
    → Recommends 3 specific tests to confirm diagnosis
    → Flags medication interaction as urgent

  Why agent here?
  - Genuinely complex, open-ended problem
  - No fixed path — followed the evidence
  - Required synthesis across many sources
  - High value task where thoroughness matters

  ---
  The Pattern Across All Scenarios

  ┌─────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐
  │     Workflow Used When      │       Agent Used When        │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ High volume, repetitive     │ Low volume, complex          │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Steps known in advance      │ Steps discovered at runtime  │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Consistency critical        │ Thoroughness critical        │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Speed and cost matter       │ Accuracy and judgment matter │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Easy to audit               │ Requires investigation       │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤
  │ Failure must be predictable │ Failure must be recoverable  │
  └─────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  The Hybrid Reality

  Most real systems use both:

  Customer contacts company
          │
          ▼
  WORKFLOW: Classify, route, handle common cases
          │
          ▼
  Is this a standard case?
          │
      ┌───┴───┐
     Yes      No
      │        │
      ▼        ▼
  WORKFLOW   AGENT
  resolves   investigates,
  it fast    adapts, resolves

  80% of cases → workflow (fast, cheap, consistent)
  20% of cases → agent (complex, edge cases, high value)

  ---
  Key Takeaway

  Workflows handle the predictable majority. Agents handle the complex exceptions. The best systems know which is which
  — and use the right tool for each.


