> explain evaulator optimizer pattren in four lines

● The evaluator-optimizer pattern uses two LLMs in a loop — one generates output, the other evaluates it and provides
  feedback. The generator then revises based on that feedback. This repeats until the evaluator approves or a maximum
  number of iterations is reached. It's like a writer and an editor working together until the output meets the quality
  bar.

> explain chaing and routing workflow in ofurlines

● Chaining breaks a complex task into sequential steps where each step's output becomes the next step's input, keeping
  each LLM call focused on one job.

  Routing classifies the input first, then directs it to the most appropriate specialized handler based on that
  classification.

  Chaining is for one path, many steps. Routing is for many paths, one step decides which.

  In practice they combine — routing picks the path, chaining handles the steps within that path.

> explain r2_score

● R² score (R-squared) measures how well a model's predictions explain the variance in the actual data, ranging from 0
  to 1 (or negative if the model is worse than a baseline).

  R² = 1 means the model perfectly predicts all values. R² = 0 means the model is no better than just predicting the
  mean every time. Negative R² means the model is actively worse than predicting the mean.

  For example, R² = 0.85 means the model explains 85% of the variance in the data — the remaining 15% is unexplained
  noise or missing features.

  It answers the question: "How much of the data's behavior does my model actually capture?"

